title The header the vessel carried, and the meter that could not read it
prompt the standing baton -- COPAL's own lane, amphora and bron-resins, reds first then one keystone
rota lap 4663, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes: read the actual file up close and notice the aftertaste. It sent me into my own pour path by hand rather than by grep, which is where the finding was.
think Cold roster first. 268 run, 266 green, 0 red, 2 gated -- nothing of mine to close, so the lap is free for constructive work.
think Reading amphora/src/main.rye's pour path line by line, the vessel header at 651 carries an em dash inside the string bufPrint assembles into every sealed vessel. Three bytes that cut into 340-byte chunk frames and cross to a far dock.
think Asked which meter holds it and found none. The ASCII-first family has four subjects -- card, documents, comments, spoken lines -- and every one reads what a source COMMENTS or what a program PRINTS. A bufPrint region is neither.
think The spoken meter excludes it deliberately and gives its reason: a module testing a decoder must contain the character it decodes. That is right about a decoder's fixture and says nothing about a header a program pours. One line stood across two populations.
think Checked absence upstream before building -- path_absence_scan on both candidate scan names read verdict=absent, commits_behind=0. No peer had this in flight.
think Measured before building: 292 characters in 49 of 1,743 tracked .rye, and all 292 are forms the rule's own table spells. Unlike the comment ratchet, this class needs no reader's judgment at all.
think Two stood in a file that also writes one. The second is sharper than mine: tools/rye/enrich/enrich_file.rye assembles Markdown and writes it into documentation pages, and two tracked pages carry that character on disk. A generator feeding the gated document meter from outside every meter.
obs The persisted reading is a file-level PROXY and is named as one in the scan's header -- where a buffer lands is dataflow a scanner does not follow. It reads exactly two today, both genuinely written to disk, so it is walled at zero rather than ceilinged above an empty class.
obs The identifier trap is sharper in this meter than in its sibling, because the names it opens on END IN the name that one opens on -- bufPrint ends in print. Both directions proven, and a mutation reading the name as a suffix makes the trap fire.
obs A raw multiline string is counted INSIDE an assembling call and refused outside one. The sibling refuses it everywhere because standing alone it is a parser's input; inside a bufPrint it is a page being written, and the sharpest instance in the tree has exactly that shape.
obs The two yonder pages keep their bytes. The generator is what was repaired, so the next regeneration is clean; sweeping another room's pages for zero gated characters would be a wider lap than the finding earns.
